course: dictionary
lesson: 5588
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have several meanings, and the words around it tell you which one the writer means. «The cook gave a liberal amount of butter to the toast.» The amount of butter is the clue. Liberal here means generous, given in large amounts.
explain: Liberal is also a political word. It describes people who believe in individual freedom, equal rights and gradual change. Politics is about how a country is run, and each side is explained in its own words, so you can read about it fairly.
explain: Always check the words next to the word. Liberal arts is a school phrase for subjects like history, languages and literature. A liberal translation is one that is not exact or strict. Ask yourself: which meaning still makes sense in this sentence?
fact: Liberal has several meanings, and the context shows which one you need.
fact: «Liberal» can mean generous, given or used in large amounts: «a liberal amount of butter».
fact: In politics, «liberal» means believing in individual freedom, equal rights and gradual change.
fact: «The Liberal Democrats are a UK party often called "the third party"; in the UK system, smaller parties can win many votes but fewer seats.»
fact: A coalition is when two or more parties share running the government, as «the Conservatives and Liberal Democrats did this in the UK from 2010 to 2015».
exercise: «The cook gave a liberal amount of butter to the toast.» What does liberal mean here? | Against freedom | *Generous, given in large amounts | Cold and hard | Strict and exact
exercise: Which of these is an example of «liberal arts» at school? | *History, languages and literature | Football practice | Swimming and gym | Maths only
exercise: «The Liberal Democrats are a UK party often called "the third party".» What does this tell you? | It is the biggest party in every country | It is a team of players | *It is a political party in the UK, and it is one of the smaller parties | It is a type of butter
test: What is a coalition? | *Two or more parties sharing the running of the government | A large amount of butter | A party that wins no votes
test: Which meaning fits «a liberal translation»? | Very large | Given in large amounts | *Not exact or strict
exam: Which meaning of liberal fits «a liberal amount of butter»? | Not exact or strict | A party in Canada | Respecting people's freedom | *Generous, given in large amounts
